{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Second BLOCK, both findings correct.\n\n(1) The plan never said how packing is induced deterministically. git gc is threshold-dependent, so it cannot be relied on to produce the shape.\n\n(2) The important one: 'removing the pack when packed' is too coarse. A repack contains the commit, trees and other event blobs the pinned view walks, so deleting it would make the view raise for a missing TREE rather than for the target ticket blob — the test would pass for the wrong reason. That is the same false-pass class as c100, in the fix for a false-pass bug.\n\nRedesigned surgically: write a pack containing ONLY the target blob (git pack-objects fed that single oid), delete the loose copy, assert git cat-file -e still resolves it so it is genuinely readable only from the pack, then delete that pack. Commit and trees stay loose and readable.\n\nAnd to prove the error is about the right object, the assertion now checks that the message names the ticket's own *-CREATE.json path. src/rebar/_snapshot/ticket_objects.py:163 raises 'missing Git object for pinned ticket path {path!r}', so the path is available to assert on rather than the generic phrase."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787906710307067001,"uuid":"1f5e1745-7072-4a18-9d50-8e163612a5cd"}